meta {
  name: 7 Set avatar
  type: http
  seq: 7
}
put {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/providers/{{providerEmail}}/avatar
  body: json
}
body:json {
  {
    "avatarId": "avatar_07"
  }
}
docs {
  Which of the 24 built-in marks this account is drawn with. `avatar_01`..`avatar_24` — the catalogue is
  `AgendaBuddy.Library/Avatars/AvatarCatalog.cs`, shared by both sides so the client cannot offer an id the
  server refuses.

  A dedicated route rather than a field on `PUT /api/v1/providers/{email}` for the same reason
  `5 Set work hours` is one — that route replaces the whole document, and doing so additionally resets every
  nested `ServiceEntity.Id` to `ObjectId.Empty` (`agenda-buddy-2wf`). Changing an avatar must not be able to
  damage a service catalogue. This one is a targeted `$set` on `avatar_id` alone.

  Answers:
  - `200` with the updated provider on success.
  - `400` for an id the catalogue does not know (try `avatar_99`). ⚠️ **Refused, never stored** — an unknown
    id throws nowhere, because `AvatarCatalog.Resolve` treats it as absent and falls back to a mark derived
    from the email address, so storing it would leave the account showing its old avatar behind a `200`.
  - `403` for another provider's email. Ownership-guarded.
  - `404` when no provider has that email.

  There is deliberately **no photo upload** yet (`agenda-buddy-5qr`). Verify with `2 Get provider by email` —
  `avatarId` comes back, and your `services` array keeps its ids.
}
